Okay, that is carried. Move on

05:58.010 --> 06:02.270
to declarations of pecuniary

06:02.770 --> 06:06.910
interest. I have a declaration for the

06:07.410 --> 06:11.670
closed committee under new

06:12.170 --> 06:16.390
business, item 4.1, disclosing a non-pecuniary conflict and

06:16.890 --> 06:22.830
will not be and did not participate in

06:23.330 --> 06:26.970
any conversation or discussion. Is there

06:27.470 --> 06:33.290
any other declarations

06:33.790 --> 06:36.410
of pecuniary interests? No. Okay.

06:36.570 --> 06:39.770
Presentations, there are no presentations. Delegations, we have five delegations, I believe, today. And for

06:40.270 --> 06:43.810
those delegations, to make sure we can hear from all delegations, we have

06:44.310 --> 06:47.450
time to properly discuss the reports on the agenda. I urge councilors to hold questions

06:47.530 --> 06:51.570
unless you have a direct and succinct

06:52.070 --> 06:55.490
question if there are questions,

06:56.210 --> 07:00.210
I will be holding firm to the limit of one question and a follow up.

07:00.690 --> 07:03.210
So we will now proceed with delegations as listed on the agenda and I would

07:03.710 --> 07:07.170
like to remind all delegations of some guidelines for participation. For those joining

07:07.670 --> 07:11.516
us in person, please proceed to the podium when you

07:12.016 --> 07:14.970
are called upon to speak. Press the button on the base of the microphone to

07:15.470 --> 07:16.810
turn it on. If the light is green, the microphone is on. For those who

07:17.310 --> 07:20.410
are joining us electronically, please leave your video camera turned off and your microphone audio

07:20.910 --> 07:23.910
muted. Until you were called upon to speak. You will have five minutes. Anyone who

07:24.410 --> 07:27.390
registered after the deadline will receive three minutes. When you see a yellow light,

07:27.890 --> 07:30.870
that means you have one minute remaining. And when you see a red light,

07:31.370 --> 07:34.563
please conclude your thoughts. If you conduct yourself in any

07:35.063 --> 07:38.630
of the following manners, it will result in an immediate removal from the meeting,

07:42.710 --> 07:46.070
interrupting counselors, staff or other delegations, derogatory statements about

07:46.570 --> 07:49.770
counselors or others, Turning your camera on at inappropriate times and any hate

07:49.850 --> 07:53.490
or defamatory comments. And for

07:53.990 --> 07:56.330
the delegation, if there are no questions, please do not hold us and please know

07:56.830 --> 08:01.450
that council is taking time to listen and

08:01.950 --> 08:05.770
reflect and, you know, process the ideas and what

08:06.270 --> 08:09.636
you are saying or speaking about. So with that, I will call on Matthew Day,

08:10.136 --> 08:13.530
Executive Director, Waterloo Region Community Energy. You will have five minutes,

08:13.770 --> 08:17.210
Matthew, thank you for Thank you for joining

08:17.710 --> 08:22.450
us this afternoon. Thank you very much

08:22.950 --> 08:26.410
for having me. Members of council, thank you for having me here

08:26.910 --> 08:30.810
today. WR Community Energy is a collaboration

08:31.310 --> 08:35.650
between the region of Waterloo, the cities of Kitchener, Waterloo, Cambridge and our local utilities.

08:36.450 --> 08:38.850
We support the energy transition in Waterloo region and we help implement the community energy

08:39.410 --> 08:43.493
investment strategy. Our local energy system is shaped by Excuse me. Thank you.

08:43.993 --> 08:45.480
Our local energy system is shaped by global economic and political forces. When we see

08:45.980 --> 08:50.360
pressures on national sovereignty and

08:50.860 --> 08:54.460
trade and affordability, we feel it here locally in housing and in infrastructure and

08:54.960 --> 08:58.400
our evolving economy. And of course, the balance between our growth

08:58.900 --> 09:02.440
and our carrying capacity in the environment. And at

09:02.940 --> 09:06.040
the same time, our energy system is shifting in tectonic ways.

09:08.530 --> 09:12.530
Slow, deep, and consequentially. And moments like

09:13.030 --> 09:16.250
this, transitions like this, invite a choice.

09:16.750 --> 09:20.410
Do we tweak the status quo or do we move forward with

09:20.910 --> 09:24.810
intention? Encouragingly, this moment is prompting thoughtful energy

09:25.310 --> 09:28.050
leadership all across Waterloo region.

09:28.770 --> 09:32.490
Last November, WR Community Energy released Shaping Our

09:32.990 --> 09:36.190
Energy Secure Future. The report highlights

09:36.430 --> 09:39.630
many forward thinking

09:40.350 --> 09:43.830
system level work across our region and the opportunity

09:44.330 --> 09:48.190
to integrate energy planning, community planning and economic development.

09:48.690 --> 09:51.870
Energy security means, yes, having access to reliable, sustainable,

09:52.270 --> 09:55.590
affordable energy insulated from global shocks, but it also means the

09:56.090 --> 09:59.870
ability to shape our future according

10:00.370 --> 10:03.790
to our values. Through local capacity and local decision-making

10:04.290 --> 10:05.870
and local benefits. Over the past 50 years, the vulnerabilities of our energy system have

10:06.370 --> 10:12.470
become clearer. Dependence on

10:12.970 --> 10:16.270
external supply chains, environmental impacts, and land use patterns that have

10:16.770 --> 10:20.270
locked us into expensive and costly infrastructure.

10:20.770 --> 10:24.590
Taken together, they point to a simple truth

10:25.090 --> 10:29.470
that cheap energy often costs

10:29.730 --> 10:33.050
A lot, including our ability to act in our own self-interest.

10:33.550 --> 10:37.570
Today, Waterloo Region imports 99% of our energy. We spend $2.1

10:38.070 --> 10:42.050
billion, and approximately $1.7 billion leaves our community every

10:42.550 --> 10:45.769
year, buying energy that we don't develop using

10:46.269 --> 10:51.130
infrastructure that we don't control. Imagine the

10:51.630 --> 10:55.650
economic impact of keeping even a portion of

10:56.150 --> 10:59.650
that here locally. WR Community Energy monitors global trends and help

11:00.150 --> 11:03.050
converts them to local action. Heating our buildings,

11:03.550 --> 11:07.210
for example, accounts for 40%

11:07.710 --> 11:11.730
of our utility energy use, and most of that heat comes from imported

11:12.230 --> 11:15.410
fossil fuels. One of our largest opportunities,

11:15.730 --> 11:20.410
both here and globally, is local clean heat. With electricity and clean

11:20.910 --> 11:25.490
energy as a catalyst,

11:25.990 --> 11:28.610
we can unlock geothermal,

11:30.740 --> 11:34.700
waste from water, waste heat from landfills, geothermal, waste heat from industry, all distributed through

11:35.200 --> 11:38.513
district energy systems when that makes sense. These are proven technologies that

11:39.013 --> 11:40.420
are already in use here in Waterloo region and across Ontario. And when combined with

11:52.260 --> 11:55.220
the modern energy grid, they can reduce emissions,

11:55.940 --> 11:59.340
increase skilled jobs, and improve resilience as well as keeping our energy dollars

11:59.840 --> 12:04.440
local. Our challenge is simply to

12:04.940 --> 12:08.800
align our energy supply with our energy demand. Finally, the energy transition

12:10.000 --> 12:14.720
cannot be delivered by

12:15.220 --> 12:19.360
any one organization or by one sector. It requires aligning policy

12:19.680 --> 12:24.800
and infrastructure and

12:25.300 --> 12:28.720
investments around a shared goal, a clean, equitable and secure energy future.

12:29.220 --> 12:32.660
Because energy security isn't just about staying warm, It's about building a future that reflects

12:32.820 --> 12:36.820
who we are, strengthens our community and develops local value. We are

12:37.320 --> 12:41.180
well positioned to act. The energy transition rewards communities

12:41.680 --> 12:46.340
that align economic, social and community benefits and

12:46.840 --> 12:49.940
collaboration is one of our strengths. We have the relationships

12:50.440 --> 12:54.060
and the expertise and the momentum. What we

12:54.560 --> 12:58.690
need is focus. I expect your council we'll

12:59.190 --> 13:02.450
see many energy opportunities in the next

13:02.950 --> 13:03.570
little while that will shape our energy future for decades to come. So finally,

13:04.070 --> 13:07.410
my ask is simple. Knowing how energy impacts our entire community, I ask that you

13:07.910 --> 13:10.090
please consider how these investments will deliver long-term value, keeping our energy dollars here,

13:10.590 --> 13:14.050
building resilience, and improving our quality

13:14.550 --> 13:16.670
of life for this generation and for futures. Thank you very much. Thank you.

13:17.170 --> 13:21.010
I am seeing no questions. Matthew, thank you for coming in and presenting to

13:21.510 --> 13:25.330
us. Always good to hear from you. Next, we have Isaac Ransom,

13:25.830 --> 13:29.250
Head of Corporate Affairs,

13:29.750 --> 13:32.970
Neuron Mobility Canada. Isaac, you will have five minutes. Good to see

13:33.470 --> 13:36.890
you again and thanks for

13:37.390 --> 13:41.930
coming in. Good afternoon. It's good to be here

13:42.430 --> 13:46.250
again. For those of you who don't know me, my name is

13:46.750 --> 13:49.570
Isaac Ransom. I'm Head of Corporate Affairs for Neuron Mobility Canada.

13:49.810 --> 13:53.530
And here to speak about the item on the agenda today in regards to

13:54.030 --> 13:57.930
the previous year of operations in

13:58.430 --> 14:02.610
the region of Waterloo. Next slide, please. So I'll keep this presentation short

14:03.110 --> 14:06.490
today in case there are questions, but we'll provide a brief overview of what

14:06.990 --> 14:10.170
last year's operations looked like. So we're starting to see the program stabilize a

14:10.670 --> 14:14.770
great deal in the region. Riders are still traveling about two

14:15.270 --> 14:19.040
kilometers a trip. And those trips are usually about

14:19.540 --> 14:23.680
10 to 12 minutes in duration. We still see

14:24.180 --> 14:27.960
a very high utilization

14:28.460 --> 14:35.400
of e-scooters with over

14:35.800 --> 14:38.960
235,000 kilometers traveled last year, which is over 127,000 trips.

14:39.460 --> 14:42.880
Rider satisfaction remains really high and we are still very present in

14:43.380 --> 14:46.800
our safety outreach in the municipality, giving away over 250 safety

14:47.300 --> 14:49.460
helmets last year. Next slide please.

14:50.820 --> 14:54.980
So at the end of each season we go out and

14:55.480 --> 14:59.100
we survey our riders to get some information from them about how

14:59.600 --> 15:03.420
they're using our service and what it means in the region.

15:03.920 --> 15:07.060
And basically some of the key metrics that we look at that are very

15:07.560 --> 15:11.380
important are vehicle replacement rate. And so we're seeing that to be very stable year

15:11.880 --> 15:14.980
over year now that we're through the second

15:15.170 --> 15:18.730
third year of the program remains around 35%. So about one in

15:19.230 --> 15:22.690
three trips are replacing an Uber or a taxi or a personal vehicle trip,

15:23.250 --> 15:27.050
which is having a significant impact on the reduction of carbon

15:27.550 --> 15:31.289
emissions in the region. We also see that Riders continue to use the service

15:31.789 --> 15:35.770
to get to work or

15:36.270 --> 15:39.970
to study, and that remains very high in the region, which is very good that

15:40.470 --> 15:44.420
there's not really much of a drop off, it's kind of within the margin

15:44.920 --> 15:48.140
of error. And then we also see that riders are combining this mode of transportation

15:48.640 --> 15:53.100
with walking. So it indicates that it's part of the active transportation practice

15:53.600 --> 15:56.699
for a lot of residents who are using the service.

15:57.199 --> 16:01.020
And we are also seeing still a very high degree of purchases when

16:01.520 --> 16:05.700
making a trip, which is having, again, a local economic impact

16:06.200 --> 16:09.780
for most riders. And most of

16:10.280 --> 16:13.500
these trips that are happening, the purchases are usually

16:13.580 --> 16:16.260
more than $10. So, you know, grabbing a coffee and something to eat, those are

16:16.760 --> 16:19.540
the sorts of purchases that are happening in each of the three municipalities participating in

16:20.040 --> 16:23.620
the program. Next slide,

16:24.120 --> 16:28.100
please. One of the major focuses for us, and I'll wrap it up

16:28.600 --> 16:33.580
after this slide, is understanding how our shared mobility program is integrating with

16:34.540 --> 16:38.260
public transit. There are three metrics that we're primarily looking at to help substantiate

16:38.760 --> 16:42.820
how riders that are integrating with the

16:43.320 --> 16:45.042
service. So the first is connecting to public transit. We can see that when we

16:45.542 --> 16:48.100
ask riders if they're connecting to transit, about one in five or about a quarter

16:48.600 --> 16:54.100
of all trips are essentially connecting with public transportation in the region. When we

16:54.600 --> 16:56.060
ask point blank if that's how people are using it on a daily basis.

16:56.560 --> 17:00.060
And again, when we re-ask the question in a different way

17:00.560 --> 17:04.100
throughout our survey on the

17:04.600 --> 17:08.430
most recent trip, we get a similar result

17:08.910 --> 17:12.590
and that matches up as well with the trips that start

17:13.090 --> 17:16.910
and end at public transportation stops.

17:17.410 --> 17:20.350
So the data that we see and where the scooters begin and where they end

17:20.850 --> 17:24.390
also correlates with the questions that we ask riders in our end of year survey.

17:24.890 --> 17:27.990
And so it indicates to us a very high utilization

17:28.490 --> 17:32.030
of e-scooters and public transit being used together in people's transportation

17:32.530 --> 17:35.460
journey. So a really interesting trend that's emerging and it's remained relatively stable year over

17:35.960 --> 17:39.526
year as we've been doing this analysis. With that, I'll keep it short.

17:40.026 --> 17:42.668
I know we're short on time today, but happy to take any questions. Thank you,

17:43.168 --> 17:46.800
Isaac, and thank you for the update. We do have a question, Councillor Hunick.

17:47.300 --> 17:50.620
Thank you, Isaac, for coming today. I just want to express my

17:51.120 --> 17:54.540
continued support for Neuron and their willingness to investigate equal opportunity

17:54.620 --> 17:58.760
solutions for folks that have

17:59.260 --> 18:03.050
different abilities and different needs. I'm well aware that The handbikes

18:03.550 --> 18:06.065
don't support everyone in our community, but they do support some. And so I wanted

18:06.565 --> 18:10.730
to draw attention to the availability of that program

18:10.970 --> 18:13.930
as well. Through you, Chair,

18:14.170 --> 18:16.970
thank you for the question, Councillor.

18:18.490 --> 18:21.210
I will just speak broadly to this and quickly. Last year, as many of you

18:21.710 --> 18:25.210
know, we launched a fleet of four supportive

18:25.370 --> 18:29.130
tricycles and two hand cycles to

18:29.630 --> 18:32.890
broaden our fleet for those with different abilities.

18:33.600 --> 18:36.800
Averaging over about a trip a week on the devices and that

18:37.920 --> 18:41.920
service is free throughout the region. So we're continuing to

18:42.420 --> 18:45.706
roll that program out and we appreciate the counselors continued support of

18:46.206 --> 18:49.679
that initiative. Thanks. Excellent, thank you Isaac.

18:49.680 --> 18:53.320
I see no further questions. Thanks for

18:53.820 --> 18:56.920
coming in and for the continued partnership with us. Appreciate it, thanks for your time.

18:57.420 --> 19:01.470
Next we have Sharon Livingstone and Rick Chambers and David Dirks, Waterloo Region

19:01.870 --> 19:05.230
Age-Friendly Network. You all have five minutes.

19:05.730 --> 19:08.190
Welcome to the region. Good to see you.

19:09.310 --> 19:12.990
Good afternoon, members of the committee. My name is

19:13.490 --> 19:17.390
Sharon Livingston. I'm chair of the Cambridge Council

19:17.890 --> 19:21.790
on Aging and a member of the Waterloo Region

19:22.030 --> 19:25.710
Age-Friendly Network. First, I want

19:26.210 --> 19:29.870
to thank council and the GRT staff for bringing forward this

19:30.110 --> 19:34.010
pilot. Offering one free transit day

19:34.510 --> 19:38.130
per week for those aged 65 and older is an

19:38.630 --> 19:42.090
important step toward making our region and community

19:42.590 --> 19:46.090
more age-friendly. Mobility is not just about transportation,

19:46.170 --> 19:49.330
it's about independence and social connection and

19:49.830 --> 19:51.770
access to essential services.

19:52.270 --> 19:56.010
In Waterloo Region, older adults

19:56.510 --> 19:59.610
now account for roughly 40%

20:00.050 --> 20:04.250
of emergency shelter occupancy. Access to reliable transportation

20:04.750 --> 20:09.010
can be the difference between maintaining stability

20:09.570 --> 20:12.450
and becoming disconnected from housing, health care,

20:12.950 --> 20:17.090
and community supports. I want to acknowledge the

20:17.410 --> 20:20.850
work staff have done in developing an implementation plan that allows for evaluation and

20:21.350 --> 20:24.690
data collection. Council needs evidence to determine whether this is an important

20:25.190 --> 20:28.950
and useful program. And that it is entirely reasonable to

20:30.550 --> 20:34.590
collect this data. However, I want to raise a

20:35.090 --> 20:38.790
concern about relying solely on the GRT pay app as the gateway for

20:39.110 --> 20:42.590
participation. The staff reports note that digital

20:43.090 --> 20:46.430
adoption among older adults is

20:46.930 --> 20:50.230
widespread, with over 85% of seniors in Ontario using the

20:50.730 --> 20:53.280
internet. And high smartphone ownership. However,

20:54.400 --> 20:58.080
using the internet and using a specific smartphone app

20:59.360 --> 21:02.986
are not the same thing. Many older adults

21:03.486 --> 21:08.000
who are comfortable checking email, reading the news, using Facebook, still face

21:08.500 --> 21:11.480
barriers when asked to download and manage a mobile app that includes registration and payments.

21:11.980 --> 21:15.120
Many older adults will face barriers related to digital literacy, disability, accessibility,

21:17.250 --> 21:21.250
or comfort with technology. Many who own smartphones rely on family,

21:21.890 --> 21:25.490
staff, or community centers and librarians to help them navigate apps

21:25.990 --> 21:29.810
and digital services. It is also important to recognize the financial

21:30.450 --> 21:34.850
realities many older adults face. Roughly 35%

21:35.350 --> 21:39.730
in this

21:40.230 --> 21:44.290
region live in poverty, meaning that they may not

21:44.790 --> 21:47.970
have reliable data plans, newer smartphones,

21:49.370 --> 21:54.010
or the digital

21:54.510 --> 21:58.050
supports needed to participate easily in an app-based program. If the

21:58.550 --> 22:02.290
goal of the pilot is to understand how free

22:02.790 --> 22:06.050
transit affects older adults, the pilot itself should

22:06.550 --> 22:10.170
not unintentionally exclude some of the people it is

22:10.670 --> 22:14.690
meant to serve. I understand that the app is attractive because

22:15.190 --> 22:18.730
it provides clean, useful data. But evaluation does not depend

22:19.130 --> 22:23.050
on an app. Transit systems already collect extensive data through automatic

22:23.130 --> 22:27.050
passenger counts, operator tallies and before

22:27.130 --> 22:28.570
and after ridership comparisons,

22:29.070 --> 22:32.890
methods used

22:33.390 --> 22:36.570
widely when evaluating fare changes. For example,

22:37.070 --> 22:40.890
other communities like Barrie and Halifax rely

22:40.970 --> 22:44.730
on simple ID verification, and aggregate ridership data

22:45.230 --> 22:49.090
rather than requiring a specific tech

22:49.590 --> 22:56.090
platform. Another concern that sometimes arises

22:56.590 --> 23:00.290
is misuses, but other

23:00.450 --> 23:04.930
transit systems address this through a reasonable ID verification programs

23:05.010 --> 23:08.490
or operator discretion, approaches that are simple, low cost, and widely

23:08.990 --> 23:10.830
used across Canada.

23:16.660 --> 23:19.540
This is why I believe a hybrid approach deserves consideration.

23:20.040 --> 23:22.180
The app can remain an option for those who prefer it and for collecting useful

23:22.680 --> 23:25.500
data, but there should also be a low barrier alternative for older adults who cannot

23:26.000 --> 23:29.940
or do not wish to use an app. This must be stressed.

23:30.440 --> 23:33.860
The success of this pilot will depend on listening directly to older adults themselves.

23:34.360 --> 23:37.920
The World Health Organization's line is Nothing about us

23:38.080 --> 23:41.280
without us. When older adults in Waterloo

23:41.360 --> 23:45.960
region were asked what matters most for aging

23:46.460 --> 23:48.320
in place, the top two issues identified were health care and transportation,

23:49.840 --> 23:54.240
both of which are among the eight domains

23:54.740 --> 23:56.800
of an age-friendly community.

23:59.680 --> 24:03.440
The age-friendly network, council and GRT staff all

24:03.940 --> 24:06.240
share the same goal. We want this pilot to be a success.

24:06.720 --> 24:10.400
We want this to be an opportunity

24:10.900 --> 24:14.280
for older adults to be meaningfully involved.

24:14.780 --> 24:18.520
Thank you. Thank you, Sharon. I do have a few questions.

24:19.020 --> 24:24.040
Stay up there. Councillor Hunick. Thank you, Chair Redman. Thank you,

24:24.540 --> 24:27.760
Chair James. Thank you for your delegation today. Thank you for pointing out the need

24:28.880 --> 24:31.120
for a hybrid option. I definitely agree with you.

24:32.730 --> 24:35.850
For many seniors who don't have access to

24:36.490 --> 24:40.170
smartphones or laptops, the ATP program cost is

24:40.670 --> 24:44.170
even accessible to them. And in my mind, the one

24:44.330 --> 24:46.690
day fair program is meant to address that. So I will be addressing that later

24:47.190 --> 24:51.610
today. Thank you very much for your insight.

24:53.610 --> 24:56.650
Thank you. Thank you. And just a reminder, it's questions for delegations. Councillor Wolfe.

25:05.780 --> 25:09.940
Through you, Madam Chair. Thank you, Sharon, for coming in. How many years have you

25:10.440 --> 25:13.380
been trying to get this motion passed? I just said to Councillor Craig,

25:13.880 --> 25:17.700
this is my 14th year. 14th time I've delegated on this

25:18.200 --> 25:21.580
