01:07:25.660 --> 01:07:29.100
policy is the proposed zero carbon building design

01:07:29.600 --> 01:07:32.780
standard, which will be presented by my colleague Max

01:07:33.280 --> 01:07:37.580
later in the presentation. Phase two, shown in orange, will focus

01:07:38.080 --> 01:07:41.260
on rapid decarbonization of regional services and infrastructure.

01:07:41.420 --> 01:07:45.580
And then phase three, shown in green, focuses on

01:07:46.080 --> 01:07:49.300
offsetting corporate emissions that cannot be eliminated, sorry,

01:07:49.800 --> 01:07:52.580
that cannot be eliminated, and that will be done through the purchase of high quality

01:07:53.080 --> 01:07:56.130
carbon credits. Next slide. So shown

01:07:56.630 --> 01:08:00.450
here on this slide is our

01:08:00.950 --> 01:08:05.850
change management approach, which is really guiding how we are

01:08:06.350 --> 01:08:10.810
tackling corporate decarbonization. So step one involved building organizational readiness,

01:08:11.310 --> 01:08:15.290
which included fostering leadership alignment through the creation of

01:08:15.790 --> 01:08:18.970
an internal governance structure, as well as

01:08:19.470 --> 01:08:23.420
establishing the imperative for action. Step two required that we developed

01:08:23.500 --> 01:08:27.180
a shared vision and roadmap, and this was achieved through the development and council

01:08:27.680 --> 01:08:31.580
endorsement of the Corporate Climate Action Plan last year,

01:08:32.080 --> 01:08:35.540
and this process was informed by extensive staff engagement and

01:08:36.040 --> 01:08:40.300
technical analysis. So we now find ourselves on

01:08:40.800 --> 01:08:45.020
step three, where we must embed climate action in corporate culture

01:08:45.520 --> 01:08:49.020
and practice. To do so, we're building tools, policies, and engagement tactics

01:08:49.540 --> 01:08:54.040
to enable staff, leadership and council to make climate informed

01:08:54.540 --> 01:08:57.980
decisions. For step four, we will monitor, report and adjust to ensure we

01:08:58.480 --> 01:09:01.740
are successful in achieving our targets and commitments.

01:09:02.240 --> 01:09:06.140
So I'll now turn the presentation over to my colleague, Kaitlyn, who will speak to

01:09:06.640 --> 01:09:10.340
our efforts to advance the corporate carbon

01:09:10.420 --> 01:09:13.900
budget. Thanks, Megan. As reported previously, we've been

01:09:14.400 --> 01:09:18.379
developing, excuse me, a corporate carbon budget which aims to link annual emissions and

01:09:18.879 --> 01:09:21.839
budget planning to long-term greenhouse gas reduction targets.

01:09:22.879 --> 01:09:26.559
Over the past year, we've conducted a comprehensive review of municipal best

01:09:27.059 --> 01:09:31.439
practice examining the efforts of Calgary, Edmonton, Toronto, and Montreal

01:09:31.939 --> 01:09:35.919
to name a few. We've completed a decarbonization

01:09:36.419 --> 01:09:39.039
pathway modeling exercise, which was shown on an earlier slide. Oh, sorry, next slide please.

01:09:39.539 --> 01:09:43.359
And we've tested a carbon budget prototype to identify a process

01:09:43.859 --> 01:09:50.399
that will suit the specific needs of our organization. Findings and associated recommendations from

01:09:50.899 --> 01:09:54.499
this work are presented on this slide. First, we heard from our municipal

01:09:54.579 --> 01:09:59.139
counterparts across Canada that corporate policies

01:09:59.299 --> 01:10:02.779
such as Green Fleet policies, zero carbon building standards, and a carbon offset policy are

01:10:03.279 --> 01:10:06.579
critical preconditions to a successful carbon budget. So we'll prioritize such policies over the next

01:10:07.079 --> 01:10:10.493
three years. Second, conducting emissions impact assessments during the annual budget

01:10:10.625 --> 01:10:15.770
process is too late to

01:10:16.270 --> 01:10:17.670
affect change. Furthermore, a unified approach is not yet possible at this time given the

01:10:18.170 --> 01:10:22.110
varied work management systems used across the region. So in recognition of

01:10:22.590 --> 01:10:26.830
these findings, we are working to embed a decarbonization targets into existing businesses

01:10:27.330 --> 01:10:31.630
and asset management planning activities. Finally, understanding the

01:10:32.130 --> 01:10:35.430
barriers faced by each business unit when implementing the core cap has proven essential and

01:10:35.930 --> 01:10:39.690
can guide the creation of annual climate priorities.

01:10:40.190 --> 01:10:43.930
Next slide, please. Informed by these findings, we are taking

01:10:44.430 --> 01:10:48.330
a phased approach to implementing the carbon

01:10:48.830 --> 01:10:52.810
budget. This slide presents the major milestones from the

01:10:53.310 --> 01:10:56.330
first phase of work. Milestones include the identification of annual critical climate priorities informed by

01:10:56.830 --> 01:11:00.850
a decarbonization barrier analysis and emission trends

01:11:01.350 --> 01:11:05.010
assessment to provide guidance for annual budget and work planning.

01:11:05.140 --> 01:11:09.540
The establishment of foundational policies that

01:11:10.040 --> 01:11:13.820
support decarbonization. Use of a robust tracking

01:11:14.320 --> 01:11:17.820
and reporting system that enables staff to understand the emissions

01:11:18.320 --> 01:11:21.259
attributed to each major program area. An annual report to council on our corporate emissions

01:11:21.759 --> 01:11:24.340
tracked against our targeted decarbonization pathway, which you can see on the blue line on

01:11:24.840 --> 01:11:28.300
this slide to the right. And efforts to integrate decarbonization targets into asset

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:33.070
management and business planning processes. Next slide,

01:11:33.570 --> 01:11:37.590
please. The second phase of the carbon budget will include the

01:11:38.090 --> 01:11:42.710
identification of annual emissions targets together with forecasted emissions reductions shown in an

01:11:43.210 --> 01:11:47.230
idealized manner on the right-hand side of the

01:11:47.730 --> 01:11:51.070
screen, which you can see in the

01:11:51.570 --> 01:11:55.750
hashed bars. This will be supported by enhancements to our

01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:59.810
decarbonization modeling, which we will conduct in 2029-2030. Next slide,

01:12:00.310 --> 01:12:03.610
please. To enable us to meet the milestones just described, we have modernized and updated

01:12:04.110 --> 01:12:08.130
our corporate greenhouse gas emissions inventory. This means we've aligned our emissions quantification processes

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:10.290
with emerging best practice and industry standards.

01:12:13.570 --> 01:12:16.450
So what do these updates mean?

01:12:16.610 --> 01:12:20.450
The graph on the right of the slide illustrates reported emissions using

01:12:20.950 --> 01:12:24.530
the original approach in blue versus the updated approach in purple.

01:12:27.230 --> 01:12:30.910
This indicates that the

01:12:31.410 --> 01:12:34.470
region generated fewer emissions between 2010 and 2013 than was previously reported.

01:12:34.970 --> 01:12:38.750
Therefore, producing an adjusted inventory baseline for 2010.

01:12:39.250 --> 01:12:42.630
The new baseline is a result of improved modeling for wastewater biosolids

01:12:43.130 --> 01:12:46.670
emissions and alignment with updated federal emissions

01:12:47.170 --> 01:12:50.670
factors. Importantly, these adjustments do not affect the core cap actions or decarbonization

01:12:51.170 --> 01:12:55.570
modeling which was based on reported emissions starting in 2022.

01:12:56.070 --> 01:13:00.010
Next slide, please. So what do we have planned for 2026? Over the

01:13:00.510 --> 01:13:04.210
first quarter, we are collecting data to assess core cap implementation progress,

01:13:04.530 --> 01:13:08.210
as well as the barriers we've faced. In the second and

01:13:08.710 --> 01:13:12.450
third quarters, we will be using these insights

01:13:12.530 --> 01:13:16.010
to launch an internal dashboard to communicate

01:13:16.510 --> 01:13:20.460
progress and emissions trends, and we will integrate key climate priorities into

01:13:20.540 --> 01:13:25.260
the 2027 annual budget and business planning process. In the fourth

01:13:25.760 --> 01:13:28.900
quarter, we will present our first annual progress

01:13:29.400 --> 01:13:32.740
report and updated emissions trends to council.

01:13:33.240 --> 01:13:36.500
Next slide, please. In addition, throughout this timeframe, we will

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:40.140
continue to explore approaches to incorporate decarbonization targets into

01:13:40.640 --> 01:13:43.900
our asset management planning and capital prioritization process and empower staff through education and training

01:13:44.400 --> 01:13:48.110
opportunities as we plan to log a climate communications engagement program.

01:13:48.610 --> 01:13:54.190
Thank you for the opportunity to

01:13:54.690 --> 01:13:58.030
share these updates with you today. And I will pass things on

01:13:58.110 --> 01:14:00.510
to my colleague, Max, corporate energy analyst,

01:14:01.629 --> 01:14:04.870
to present the zero carbon building standard. Okay, just want to update council

01:14:05.370 --> 01:14:08.990
that this presentation as well deals with the following report 8.2.2,

01:14:09.230 --> 01:14:12.790
correct? So just for council's awareness. Thank you so much.

01:14:12.950 --> 01:14:16.190
Thank you for having me here

01:14:16.690 --> 01:14:20.110
today. My name is Max Chute. I'm a corporate energy analyst here

01:14:20.610 --> 01:14:23.750
at the region and I'm excited to present on the zero carbon building design standard.

01:14:24.150 --> 01:14:27.509
Next slide please. So the purpose of this report is to align with the

01:14:28.009 --> 01:14:30.950
region's targets of zero carbon emissions by 2050. To do so, the staff are recommending

01:14:31.030 --> 01:14:34.390
a new building standard. Our existing standard is LEED Silver,

01:14:34.890 --> 01:14:38.680
which council adopted in 2005. LEED stands for Leadership

01:14:39.180 --> 01:14:41.680
in Energy and Environmental Design, and Silver is one of the four tiers of certification

01:14:42.180 --> 01:14:46.120
you can achieve. LEED is focused on achieving sustainability through various

01:14:46.620 --> 01:14:50.080
design and construction practices, including some energy efficiency and reduced carbon emissions,

01:14:50.580 --> 01:14:54.720
but it focuses on a more broad sustainability framework without requiring significant

01:14:54.800 --> 01:14:59.680
energy or carbon reductions. Staff are proposing the ZCB Design Standard or Zero

01:14:59.840 --> 01:15:03.450
Carbon Building Design standard,

01:15:03.950 --> 01:15:08.210
which was created by the Canada Green Building Council in

01:15:08.710 --> 01:15:12.850
2017. This standard aims to design buildings with net zero operational carbon

01:15:13.350 --> 01:15:17.330
balances and reduced embodied carbon. It prioritizes high energy efficiency

01:15:17.490 --> 01:15:21.070
and on-site renewable energy, and it includes future climate

01:15:21.570 --> 01:15:25.730
data analysis to support climate adaptation. And other parts of

01:15:26.230 --> 01:15:29.850
the buildings are inherently more climate resilient, such as the high performance envelopes,

01:15:30.350 --> 01:15:33.500
which are which are helpful in cases of long power outages

01:15:34.000 --> 01:15:37.660
to maintain indoor temperatures. Targeting the ZCB design standard ensures

01:15:38.160 --> 01:15:41.780
that new buildings are aligned with the region's 2050 net zero emissions goal.

01:15:42.280 --> 01:15:45.780
Next slide please. So comparing

01:15:46.280 --> 01:15:50.300
these two standards, we see, as I said, the LEED

01:15:50.800 --> 01:15:54.780
Silver is a broad sustainability standard with

01:15:55.280 --> 01:15:58.770
many categories including energy, water, site location, materials, indoor air quality,

01:15:59.270 --> 01:16:03.370
innovation, et cetera. It is

01:16:03.870 --> 01:16:07.730
a point-based scoring system where if you achieve between 50 and 59 points out

01:16:08.230 --> 01:16:12.044
of a total of 110, you achieve the LEED Silver certification. I'll note that it

01:16:12.544 --> 01:16:16.690
is possible to design a LEED Silver building that does not make any improvements

01:16:16.850 --> 01:16:20.650
to energy or carbon reductions and still achieve

01:16:21.150 --> 01:16:24.450
LEED Silver. Based on that, energy is just one of many scoring

01:16:24.950 --> 01:16:28.910
criteria. The ZCB design policy, on the

01:16:29.410 --> 01:16:32.870
other hand, is a carbon-focused certification that directly targets carbon

01:16:33.370 --> 01:16:38.270
reductions and energy efficiency. Again, the three key points are very

01:16:38.770 --> 01:16:42.430
strict requirements for design zero operational carbon balances, a reduced

01:16:42.930 --> 01:16:46.270
embodied carbon and high energy efficiency, and it is a pass/fail

01:16:46.770 --> 01:16:50.150
criteria which is easier to administer and work through first staff. Next slide, please.

01:16:51.200 --> 01:16:54.480
The capital cost premium of a zero carbon building can be between 0

01:16:54.980 --> 01:16:58.520
and 7% as per the Climate Action Partnership's recent briefing

01:16:59.020 --> 01:17:03.760
note on high performance building costing studies. Operational savings associated with this high

01:17:03.920 --> 01:17:08.000
energy efficiency often can cover that initial

01:17:08.240 --> 01:17:11.840
capital premium over the lifespan of the building. In regional housing, this can lead to

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:15.520
lower energy costs and ultimately increasing affordability of living in the regional

01:17:16.400 --> 01:17:20.830
housing as those tenants pay either directly or

01:17:21.330 --> 01:17:24.750
indirectly for the utility costs of the buildings they reside in. It also supports cost

01:17:25.250 --> 01:17:28.670
effective capital planning by building to the

01:17:28.990 --> 01:17:32.110
2050 net zero emissions target now. We avoid

01:17:32.350 --> 01:17:35.750
expensive retrofits later on, as well as avoiding the 20

01:17:36.250 --> 01:17:39.790
years of greenhouse gas emissions that those

01:17:39.870 --> 01:17:43.950
systems would emit before we retrofit them at

01:17:44.430 --> 01:17:47.750
end of life. Finally, it enables access to federal infrastructure funding that is

01:17:48.250 --> 01:17:51.610
tied to net zero buildings, and that can further help offset capital costs. Next slide.

01:17:52.110 --> 01:17:54.930
So staff are recommending that we move from the LEED Silver standard that was adopted

01:17:55.430 --> 01:17:59.370
by the region in 2005 and is a broader sustainability

01:17:59.870 --> 01:18:03.210
standard to the Zero Carbon Building Design standard that focuses

01:18:03.710 --> 01:18:07.850
on high energy efficiency, eliminating operational carbon emissions, and reducing

01:18:08.090 --> 01:18:11.810
embodied carbon to align with

01:18:12.310 --> 01:18:15.570
the corporate climate action plan and the region's 2050

01:18:16.070 --> 01:18:19.570
net zero GHG emissions objective. Thank you. Thank you. Okay,

01:18:20.070 --> 01:18:24.130
is there another presenter, Danielle? Okay. So we have, there's the

01:18:24.630 --> 01:18:27.610
two reports. So 8.2.1, that is for information,

01:18:28.110 --> 01:18:33.250
but there is

01:18:33.750 --> 01:18:37.570
a recommendation for 8.2.2. So we will need a

01:18:38.070 --> 01:18:41.530
mover and seconder for that. I do see a question,

01:18:42.030 --> 01:18:45.360
Councilor Wolfe, will you move it? Councillor Fox and you'll second it. Okay,

01:18:45.440 --> 01:18:48.800
so we will go to questions for staff. Councillor Hunic, you're up.

01:18:49.300 --> 01:18:56.640
Chair James, I have a question for 8.2.1

01:18:57.140 --> 01:19:00.920
and then a comment for 8.2.2. My question

01:19:01.420 --> 01:19:04.880
for 8.2.1 is this indicates really great work on behalf of regional staff

01:19:05.380 --> 01:19:08.680
and also on behalf of the residents throughout our region and progress, I think,

01:19:10.350 --> 01:19:13.910
in the right direction. My question might be a little weird

01:19:14.410 --> 01:19:18.110
or backwards. I'm wondering if you have any

01:19:18.510 --> 01:19:22.070
insight into weak points or areas of focus to

01:19:22.570 --> 01:19:27.870
move toward for the future, be it this council or the newly elected council

01:19:28.370 --> 01:19:31.990
one year from now. Through the chair. It's a great

01:19:32.490 --> 01:19:36.350
and big question, yes. So what I will say is we typically divide

01:19:36.850 --> 01:19:39.670
our consideration of emissions into corporate emissions emissions and community emissions. So this really is

01:19:40.170 --> 01:19:46.390
focused on corporate emissions.

01:19:46.890 --> 01:19:49.590
And so I think it's fair to say our big areas of focus are providing

01:19:50.090 --> 01:19:53.710
EV charging infrastructure. We're excited to really move forward aggressively with that

01:19:54.190 --> 01:19:57.790
