01:32:02.830 --> 01:32:06.210
has an urban forestry section under the

01:32:06.710 --> 01:32:10.450
transportation department which oversees the management of individual trees in primarily road

01:32:10.950 --> 01:32:14.970
right of ways and regional facilities. However, we do collaborate quite closely

01:32:15.470 --> 01:32:18.400
with this team and ensure that we're taking a consistent and complementary approach to protecting

01:32:18.900 --> 01:32:22.880
and enhancement of our green infrastructure assets. Thank you. The region currently

01:32:23.380 --> 01:32:27.240
owns 17 regional forests amounting close to 500 hectares of land. Most of the

01:32:27.740 --> 01:32:31.480
portfolio was acquired between the

01:32:31.980 --> 01:32:35.760
1940s to the early 1950s in an effort to

01:32:36.260 --> 01:32:40.120
reverse the large scale deforestation that occurred in southern

01:32:40.620 --> 01:32:43.528
Ontario, most notably blow sands and flooding. Most of our network is forested, however we

01:32:44.325 --> 01:32:45.910
Now, I'd like to share a handful of program highlights from 2025. We planted trees

01:32:46.630 --> 01:32:50.870
on multiple sites, including a notable four and a half hectare afforestation site

01:32:51.370 --> 01:32:55.590
at the Waterloo Landfill Regional Forest. We prioritized tree marking

01:32:56.090 --> 01:32:59.510
to identify trees for harvest or protection as part of our ongoing efforts to

01:33:00.010 --> 01:33:02.690
increase forest resilience and health. We also conducted targeted invasive species management with an emphasis

01:33:03.190 --> 01:33:07.350
on the treatment of common buckthorn and giant hogweed. And finally, we conducted

01:33:07.850 --> 01:33:11.790
forest health surveys to inform management prescriptions for pests and disease

01:33:12.290 --> 01:33:16.310
cycles and forest health threats

01:33:16.810 --> 01:33:19.830
including spongy moth, beech bark disease,

01:33:20.330 --> 01:33:24.150
hemlock woolly adelgid and oak wilt among many other.

01:33:24.650 --> 01:33:28.430
Regardless to say it was a busy year. Next slide please.

01:33:28.930 --> 01:33:32.150
I'll shift now to our priorities for 2026. This year we've

01:33:32.650 --> 01:33:35.390
initiated the renewal of our regional forest management plan.

01:33:35.890 --> 01:33:39.030
The current plan was approved by regional council in 2006

01:33:39.530 --> 01:33:43.070
for the 2007-2012 and 7 to 2026 operating period. It has entered, consequently,

01:33:43.570 --> 01:33:48.470
its last year of its planning cycle. Since that time, we've observed

01:33:48.970 --> 01:33:52.390
significant changes in our forests in response to existing and emerging challenges.

01:33:52.470 --> 01:33:56.309
Here, I've included a snapshot of some of these

01:33:56.809 --> 01:34:00.830
challenges, which include an increase in damage caused

01:34:01.330 --> 01:34:04.910
by extreme weather events, novel forest pests and

01:34:05.410 --> 01:34:09.190
pathogens, the continued spread of exotic invasive species such as dog strangling vine, and common

01:34:09.690 --> 01:34:13.190
buckthorn, and a growing user demand as our community accessed the forests more frequently.

01:34:13.690 --> 01:34:15.878
So we know we need to update our management strategies to address these challenges.

01:34:16.378 --> 01:34:18.750
At the same time, there are some exciting opportunities that we can capitalize on.

01:34:19.250 --> 01:34:22.950
These include new technologies which will allow us to enhance our understanding of forced conditions,

01:34:23.450 --> 01:34:27.350
strong public interest and support for these critical assets, land co-stewardship partnerships with our First

01:34:27.850 --> 01:34:31.750
Nations and Indigenous communities that

01:34:32.250 --> 01:34:34.950
advance our reconciliation efforts,

01:34:35.440 --> 01:34:39.440
and new education models that among

01:34:39.940 --> 01:34:43.040
other things bring kids into nature.

01:34:43.840 --> 01:34:44.640
